 2. Exclusive: Murdock makin&#x27; moves 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 A U.S. Air Force KC-46A Pegasus approaches Travis Air Force Base, California. Photo: Kenneth Abbate/DVIDS

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Murdock, a portable pilot assistant made by Beacon AI, has flown on three U.S. Air Force refueler-and-transporter aircraft — advising flight crews and racking up hours and hours of real-world experience.

 Why it matters: The California startup is building "R2-D2 for pilots," according to co-founder Matt Cox, a former Navy fighter pilot, and this is a significant step in that direction.

 "These planes are very instrumented. There are thousands and thousands of parameters. We consume it," he told me. Driving the news: Murdock was used aboard five KC-46A flights, beginning with the Emerald Flag drills at Eglin Air Force Base , Florida.

 The work was conducted under a contract with Air Mobility Command and followed an earlier deal with Special Operations Command. "Our system is similar to what Tesla Autopilot would be," Cox said, "if it was its own company." How it works: Murdock pulls data from avionics buses and presents information that helps crews complete checklists and procedures, like taxiing and taking off; plan routes; and keep tabs on personal readiness, including fatigue levels.

 It can be carried onto an aircraft in a Pelican case and operates without modifying flight-critical systems. "Our company is very aimed at supporting pilot monitoring," Cox said. "The feedback has been, 'Good job. Keep going, go do more,'" he added. "AMC is really leaning in on these connectivity efforts." Zoom out: The U.S. military has for years sought to augment troops with smart machinery and to predict when, where and how things will break. 

 Follow the money: Beacon raised $15 million in late 2024. The Series A was led by Costanoa Ventures. 

 OpenAI's Sam Altman is among the startup's earliest backers. What's next: Murdock is expected to fly aboard two additional aircraft: another "big one," Cox said, and one that is "smaller."

 "We want exposure to lots of different things," he said. "In aviation, some stuff happens every 100,000 hours — or every 1,000,000 hours." Go deeper: Inside Red Flag: U.S. and allies train for future air combat 

 
 
